WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.798
Dynamic memory allocation lets C++ programs create objects and buffers whose size or lifetime is not known at compile time.

2
00:00:07.798 --> 00:00:14.037
That flexibility is useful in parsers, services, embedded software, desktop applications, libraries, and performance-sensitive systems.

3
00:00:14.037 --> 00:00:18.326
It lets software adapt to input, workload, configuration, and resource availability.

4
00:00:18.326 --> 00:00:20.275
That flexibility also creates responsibility.

5
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:25.734
If ownership is unclear, memory can remain allocated after it is no longer needed.

6
00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:30.413
If cleanup happens too early, later code may continue using stale access.

7
00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:40.941
If size calculations are wrong, the program may request too little memory, too much memory, or a shape of memory that does not match the data being

8
00:00:40.941 --> 00:00:41.331
stored.

9
00:00:41.331 --> 00:00:46.790
Dynamic allocation is safest when the lifecycle is designed before the first allocation happens.

10
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:56.147
The code should identify who owns the object, who may borrow it, whether ownership can transfer, whether sharing is required, and what cleanup rule

11
00:00:56.147 --> 00:00:56.537
applies.

12
00:00:56.537 --> 00:01:00.826
Those answers should be visible in types, APIs, and class design.

13
00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:03.166
Manual memory management should be minimized.

14
00:01:03.166 --> 00:01:09.794
Modern C++ provides values, containers, RAII wrappers, and smart pointers that keep cleanup tied to object lifetime.

15
00:01:09.794 --> 00:01:14.863
These tools reduce repeated manual cleanup paths and make ownership easier to review.

16
00:01:14.863 --> 00:01:17.203
The course theme is preventive engineering.

17
00:01:17.203 --> 00:01:23.831
Safe allocation starts with ownership, lifetime, size validation, initialization, cleanup, failure handling, resource limits, diagnostics, and review.

18
00:01:23.831 --> 00:01:29.680
Cleanup after the fact is much harder than designing the lifecycle clearly from the beginning.
